    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "What is a deferred tax liability, and how does accelerated tax depreciation create one?",
    a: "A deferred tax liability is tax postponed to later years because the company has so far reported less taxable income than book income. With accelerated tax depreciation, tax deductions run ahead of book depreciation early on, so cash taxes fall below the income statement's tax expense. The gap builds a DTL, which reverses as tax depreciation drops below book.",
    why: "Companies keep two sets of books, legitimately: GAAP statements for investors and a tax return for the government. Book tax expense is based on book income; cash taxes on taxable income. Depreciation differs only in timing, since both methods deduct the same total cost over the asset's life, so the tax saved now is repaid later and is recorded as a liability. On the cash flow statement, the increase in the DTL is added back as the non-cash part of tax expense.",
    formula: "DTL = (cumulative tax depreciation − cumulative book depreciation) × tax rate\nBook tax expense = cash taxes + increase in DTL",
    example: "A [[$100M]] machine is depreciated straight-line over [[5]] years for book, $20M a year, while tax depreciation is [[$40M]] in year 1. Taxable income is $20M below book income, so at [[25%]] cash taxes are $5M below book tax expense and a $5M DTL is recorded. When tax depreciation later falls below $20M a year, the DTL unwinds.",
    trap: "A growing company keeps buying assets, so new deferrals offset reversals and the DTL can grow for years. That's why some analysts treat it as closer to equity than debt. The three-statement walk-through is the usual follow-up."
