    q: "What creates a deferred tax asset? Give three common sources.",
    a: "A deferred tax asset arises when a company has booked an expense or loss it can deduct for tax only later, so its future taxes will be lower. Three common sources: net operating losses carried forward; reserves such as warranty or bad-debt allowances, deductible only when paid or written off; and stock comp, expensed before the tax deduction arrives.",
    why: "It's the mirror image of a DTL: the books have already recognized a cost or loss that will cut taxable income later. With a reserve, the company pays more tax now than its income statement suggests and recovers it when the deduction arrives; with an NOL, past losses shelter future profits. The asset equals the future deduction × the tax rate. Unlike a DTL, it's worth something only if the company earns enough taxable income to use it, which is why DTAs, not DTLs, are tested for realizability.",
    example: "A company builds an [[$8M]] allowance for doubtful accounts, but tax deducts bad debts only when they're written off. Taxable income is $8M above book income, so at [[25%]] it pays $2M more tax than its income statement shows and records a $2M DTA. When the receivables are written off and deducted, the DTA reverses.",
