    type: "qa",
    q: "What's the difference between a temporary and a permanent book-tax difference, and why does only one of them create deferred taxes?",
    a: "A temporary difference reverses: the item hits book and taxable income in different years but in the same total, like depreciation or accrued expenses, so it creates deferred taxes. A permanent difference never reverses, like a fine that's never deductible, tax-exempt interest or a tax credit. With nothing to catch up later, it changes the effective tax rate instead.",
    why: "Deferred taxes exist to match tax expense with book income over time. When a difference will reverse, the tax effect is only postponed, so a DTA or DTL records it and book tax expense stays at the statutory rate × book income. When it never reverses, there's nothing to defer: the company pays more or less tax on its book income for good, and the effective rate moves. Goodwill impairment in a stock deal is the classic interview case: never deductible, so no tax saving at all.",
    formula: "Effective tax rate = book tax expense ÷ pre-tax book income\nBook tax expense = current (cash) tax + deferred tax expense",
    example: "Pre-tax book income [[$100M]], tax rate [[25%]]. A [[$4M]] non-deductible fine and [[$10M]] of extra tax depreciation give taxable income of $94M and current tax of $23.5M. Deferred tax expense is $2.5M, the DTL on the depreciation, so book tax expense is $26M: a 26% effective rate. The fine moved the rate; the depreciation didn't.",
    trap: "Don't say the effective rate differs from the statutory rate because of deferred taxes. Temporary differences leave it unchanged; permanent items, credits, state and foreign rates and valuation allowance changes move it."
