    why: "It's hard to tell which spending creates a brand, or what it's worth, so US GAAP and IFRS both expense it; IFRS explicitly bans capitalizing homegrown brands and customer lists. A purchase supplies a price. The result distorts comparisons: a serial acquirer shows more assets, amortization and goodwill than an organic grower with the same economics, so its return on assets looks lower, while the grower's investment runs through operating expenses. That's one reason bankers lean on EBITDA and cash flow rather than book returns.",
