    q: "How is a convertible bond accounted for under US GAAP today, and why does IFRS report higher interest expense on the same bond?",
    a: "Since ASU 2020-06, US GAAP usually records a convertible as a single liability at amortized cost, so interest expense stays near the low coupon. IFRS splits it: a liability valued like straight debt at a market rate, plus an equity component for the conversion option. The liability's discount is amortized, so IFRS interest expense approaches a non-convertible bond's rate.",
